 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Output code",
   "expr": "Code = 2^(N−1) × ((VINx / VREF) + 1)",
   "where": {
    "VINx": "differential analog input voltage",
    "VREF": "reference voltage, REFIN(+) − REFIN(−)",
    "N": "16 for the AD7171"
   },
   "note": "Offset binary: −VREF gives 000...000, 0 V gives 100...000, +VREF gives 111...111.",
   "page": 12
  },
  {
   "name": "Effective number of bits",
   "expr": "ENOB = ln(FSR / RMS noise) / ln(2)",
   "where": {
    "FSR": "full-scale range, 2 × VREF / gain"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 10
  },
  {
   "name": "Noise-free bits",
   "expr": "Noise-Free Bits = ln(FSR / Peak-to-Peak Noise) / ln(2)",
   "where": {
    "FSR": "full-scale range, 2 × VREF / gain"
   },
   "note": "Equal to the p-p resolution: the resolution with no code flicker.",
   "page": 10
  },
  {
   "name": "Conversion period",
   "expr": "t = 1 / fADC = 1 / 125 Hz = 8 ms",
   "where": {
    "fADC": "output data rate, 125 Hz"
   },
   "note": "Settling time is 3/fADC = 24 ms.",
   "page": 11
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Filter and settling",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "A sinc3 filter sets a fixed 125 Hz rate, one result every 8 ms. The first result after a reset takes the full 24 ms settling time; later ones come every 8 ms. After a step at the input, a step aligned with the conversion period needs one settling time before a result is valid; a step that lands mid-conversion needs one conversion more. Every conversion updates the register regardless. The filter has nulls at multiples of 125 Hz; a simple RC on the inputs is the only external filtering needed, to reject energy near multiples of the master clock."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Gain and input range",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Gain is fixed at 1, so the differential range is ±VREF (±5 V with a 5 V reference). The input is bipolar only in the differential sense: AIN(+) is measured against AIN(−), and neither pin may leave GND − 30 mV to VDD + 30 mV, or linearity and noise degrade. With AIN(−) at 2.5 V and a 2.5 V reference, AIN(+) can swing 0 V to 5 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Unbuffered inputs",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "The analog input connects directly to the modulator, so it presents a switching load to its driver. Series R and shunt C on the inputs give a dc gain error that depends on the source impedance; the external RC table lists the pairs that add no error at 16 bits."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reference",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "The reference input is differential and unbuffered with a common-mode range of GND to VDD. Nominal REFIN is VDD; the part works from 0.5 V to VDD. Source RC on the reference causes gain error. A ratiometric hookup, where the sensor excitation also makes the reference, cancels low-frequency excitation noise; otherwise a low-noise reference is needed. The ADR381 and ADR391 are low-noise, low-power 2.5 V references that suit the part; with their low output impedance, a decoupling capacitor on REFIN(+) adds no gain error. When the reference is taken across an external resistor, the source impedance is high and REFIN decoupling is advised against."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power-down and reset",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Holding PDRST low for at least 100 ns powers down everything, clock included, tristates DOUT/RDY and resets the interface, digital filter and modulator. Releasing it high starts the clock (about 1 ms), then the modulator samples; DOUT/RDY goes high and stays high until the first valid result, which takes the full settling time (25 ms typ from PDRST high to data valid). The part also resets itself at power-up."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Serial read",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Two wires: SCLK in and DOUT/RDY out. DOUT/RDY falls when a new word is ready; SCLK pulses then shift out 24 bits, the 16-bit result MSB first followed by eight status bits. Data changes on the SCLK falling edge. Reading the word sets DOUT/RDY high again. Each result can be read only once. If a result is not read, DOUT/RDY rises before the next register update so that no read starts during it; the whole word must be read before the next conversion finishes, since the interface resets when a new result lands."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Status check",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "PAT2 to PAT0 read 101 by default; any other pattern means the serial transfer was corrupted. ID1, ID0 read 0, 1 for the AD7171; the 12-bit AD7170 is pin-compatible with the same features. ERR flags an input out of range."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Grounding and layout",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Since both inputs are differential, most modulator voltages are common-mode and are rejected; the digital filter also rejects broadband supply noise except at whole multiples of the modulator sampling frequency. On the board, analog and digital circuitry occupy separate regions, and a minimum-etch ground plane gives the best shielding and extends beneath the part. GND goes to the system analog ground, and the single analog-to-digital ground join sits near the AD7171. Each return current flows close to its outgoing path, and no digital current crosses the analog ground. Supply traces are as wide as practical. Clocks and other fast edges are shielded with digital ground and kept clear of the analog inputs; analog and digital signals do not cross, and traces on the two board faces meet at right angles. A microstrip layout, ground plane on the component side and signals on the solder side, works best. VDD takes a 10 µF tantalum and a 0.1 µF capacitor in parallel to GND, mounted as close to the part as possible; each logic chip takes a 0.1 µF ceramic to DGND."
  }
 ],
